[% set v = c.values_ref %]
[% set h = c.helper %]
{{- /*
===============================================================================
ConfigMap of the "[[ c.name ]]" component
===============================================================================
File generated by forge.

Carries the NON sensitive configuration of [[ c.name ]]. Nothing secret must pass
through here: a ConfigMap is readable by anyone who has the right to read the
resources of the namespace. Sensitive values go into the Secret of the component.

Two injection modes, chosen by the "mountAs" key of values.yaml:
  env  : the whole ConfigMap is injected as environment variables, via envFrom on
         the container;
  file : the ConfigMap is mounted under "mountPath", one file per key — the name
         of the file is the key, its content the value.

The name of this template file is constrained: the pod template of the workload
computes the "checksum/config" fingerprint by quoting "configmap-[[ c.name ]].yaml"
literally. It is that fingerprint which makes the pods restart when the
configuration changes — without it, a "helm upgrade" that only touches this
ConfigMap would leave the pods on the old configuration, without the slightest
sign. Renaming this file would make the rendering of the workload fail on a
template that cannot be found.

No value is hardcoded here: everything comes from the "[[ c.name ]].config" key
of values.yaml, overridable per environment.
===============================================================================
*/}}
{{- if and [[ v ]].enabled [[ v ]].config.enabled }}
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: {{ include "[[ h ]].configMapName" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "[[ h ]].labels" . | nindent 4 }}
{{- /*
  The "data" block is only rendered if it has content: a "data:" key followed by
  nothing amounts to null in YAML, and null is refused where the schema expects a
  table of strings. With no declared key, "{}" is therefore written explicitly.

  Keys AND values go through "quote":
    - an unquoted value (8080, true, 1.5) would come out as an integer, a boolean
      or a float, whereas data only accepts strings: refused in strict mode;
    - an unquoted key suffers the same fate, and "on" or "yes" would even become
      booleans under YAML 1.1 parsers.
  The "range" iteration over a Go table is done by sorted key: the output is
  therefore stable from one render to the next.
*/}}
{{- with [[ v ]].config.data }}
data:
  {{- range $key, $value := . }}
  {{ $key | quote }}: {{ $value | quote }}
  {{- end }}
{{- else }}
data: {}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
